<p>1960</p>

<p>Mandan experienced a resurgence in civic service, with record numbers of fraternal and service clubs and members. This was evident in the number and size of New Year's parties at the downtown Elks Building, Moose Home, Knights of Columbus Hall, American Legion Hall, Veterans of Foreign Wars Club, and Eagles Club. The Revelers Club celebrated at the Mandan Country Club.</p>

<p>After several earlier attempts, Mandan residents approved a move to year-round Central Standard Time by a margin of almost two to one, effective May 15.</p>

<p>A record temperature of 74°F on March 28 hastened the breakup of Missouri River ice on April 5. A huge ice jam on the Heart River backed water over Highway 10 to a depth of 2 feet, forcing the floodgates to close and stopping all traffic to Bismarck. More than 400 Republican convention delegates were staying in Mandan, so the Northern Pacific Railway added six special commuter trains between Mandan and Bismarck to transport them to and from the convention center.</p>

<p>A new gymnasium and additional classrooms were built on the north side of Mandan Senior High School. The gym in the $290,000 addition seated more than 2,000 people for basketball games and included storage and complete athletic facilities, including showers and lockers.</p>

<p>1961</p>

<p>At a time when stay-at-home mothers were common, Morton County had at least five homemakers clubs: the Rock Haven Homemakers Club, Almont Homemakers Club, Fort McKeen Club, Big Bend Homemakers Club, and Highland Homemakers Club.</p>

<p>1962</p>

<p>In April, Mandan elected its first woman to the City Commission. Dolores Pierce joined incumbent George Schantz, who won reelection.</p>

<p>1963</p>

