  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1960s/144-cd-logo.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Grooving through the Sixties 1960-1969 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Six community civil defense shelters were established in Morton County in April, including four within Mandan's city limits. The Mandan fallout shelters were in the basements of the Lewis and Clark Hotel, Mandan Creamery, St. Joseph School, and the courthouse. Black Motor Sales in New Salem and Hebron Motors Inc. in Hebron hosted shelters for rural areas of the county. Each could hold as many as 1,100 people. The Department of Defense supplied cartons of rations and sanitation and medical supplies.</p>

<p>1964</p>

<p>Direct Distance Dialing came to Mandan in July. A $1.5 million Northwestern Bell Telephone Company upgrade brought seven-digit dialing to the city one month after the same service began in Bismarck. The system replaced the three- and four-digit telephone numbers previously in use. "663" was Mandan's first exchange prefix.</p>

<p>1965</p>

<p>In February, firefighters battled for three hours to extinguish a major fire at the historic Merchant Hotel site at 106 East Main Street. The fire began in the basement and, driven by high winds, quickly spread to both adjacent commercial buildings. At the time, the former hotel housed Ray's Bootery, owned and operated by Ray Mosbrucker. Other affected properties included Seb's Shoe Repair and the vacant former Friesz Grocery building.</p>

