  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1960s/274-grant-marsh-bridge.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Grooving through the Sixties 1960-1969 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Interstate 94 was completed, including a second river crossing on the Grant Marsh Bridge. As in many North Dakota cities, traffic was diverted from Main Street and North Dakota Highway 10. The downtown business districts on both sides of the river eventually suffered as retail stores migrated to stand-alone shopping centers and commercial areas along interstate routes.</p>

<p>1966</p>

<p>North Dakota experienced the "Storm of the Century" from March 2 through March 4, when a late-winter blizzard struck the state. More than 22 inches of snow fell in the area. The <em>Mandan Pioneer</em> published a special souvenir edition about the event.</p>

<p><a href="/assets/documents/area-history/mandan-pioneer-1966-snow-of-the-century-derated.pdf">&#x27;66 Blizzard Souvenir Edition</a></p>

<p>The era of railroad steam engines receded further into the past when demolition experts blasted down a 165-foot chimney in the Mandan rail yards. The Heine Chimney Company had built the reinforced-concrete stack in 1928 to provide draft for steam-engine boilers and returned to topple it with dynamite.</p>

<p>1967</p>

<p>Work was completed in January on the relocation of the Northern Pacific's Mandan-to-Flasher main line. The {% inflation 10000000, 1967 %} project involved more than 40 miles of new track installed by two work crews, which met near Fallon. Construction had begun in September 1965. The U.S. Army Corps of Engineers initiated the project to move the line away from the effects of Lake Oahe. The new route was 22 miles shorter than the old track, which meandered for 67 miles along the Cannonball and Missouri rivers. The original line was abandoned that spring.</p>

